 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Two-terminal operation",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Reverse current enters the cathode and leaves the anode; the voltage across the part is the bandgap reference. The operating current range is 10 µA to 20 mA. Below about 1.2 V (1.2 V parts) or 2.5 V (2.5 V parts) across it, the reverse current is a few µA or less (Figures 2 and 8)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Voltage versions and grades",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Two voltages are made, 1.235 V (suffix -1.2) and 2.500 V (suffix -2.5), set by on-chip trimming: one link on the die is opened for the 1.235 V version and another for the 2.5 V version (Figure 1). Initial tolerance grades are 1.0 %, 1.5 %, 2.0 % and 3.0 %: the LM285 and LM385B hold ±1.0 % (1.2 V) or ±1.5 % (2.5 V) at 25 °C, and the LM385 limits are wider."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature ranges",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "LM285 parts are specified over −40 °C to +85 °C; LM385 and LM385B over 0 °C to +70 °C. The junction may reach +150 °C and storage runs −65 °C to +150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Forward conduction",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "With forward bias the part conducts; forward current is rated to 10 mA, with roughly 0.25 V to 1.0 V across it from 10 µA to 20 mA depending on temperature (Figures 4 and 10)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Response time",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "With a step of about 10 V applied through 100 kΩ, the output overshoots and settles to the reference voltage within about 0.25 ms; when the input returns to 0 V the output decays to near 0 V over roughly 0.4 ms (Figures 7 and 13)."
  }
 ],
